#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dffdd80-025d-50d1-ac37-c8f164bdf66b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/24/24e5c8753de2c0376dcf9832d96a44e3b33890d5ffb46647bc1f376a733f414a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387769} {:answer/id #uuid "5f007014-ee86-5f05-9e67-582ead30871b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f0495fdf0b97d435d4f931d07ef97cf1fe97f1ead509c7e07eb503fadba36392.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387770} {:answer/id #uuid "035d28b1-01a1-5399-8f7b-76cb46849927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bd/bd696f83cd0b77167aeb249e540e09e05a869435a83854996882b495006a58bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387771} {:answer/id #uuid "87ce4db1-8106-5437-ae1f-f0f8f5398f11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6b/6b80bd605297face2f098b9e5310dc5c5e892da323c89488424457685b6714da.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387772} {:answer/id #uuid "475af506-765b-539b-bfe5-00c3bec282f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9c744c4d1453d6a397f1a2510213c397918ad913d7f4247d2b808119acdc9b9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "035d28b1-01a1-5399-8f7b-76cb46849927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bd/bd696f83cd0b77167aeb249e540e09e05a869435a83854996882b495006a58bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387771} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b59eb53-3413-5716-8fda-ec3e073c567a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95fb30a5-3c2e-5dd6-9b82-b24be8784858" :question/math-academy-id "q-127008" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,5]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,5],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,5]$ and differentiable on $(0,5).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[0,5]$ and differentiable on $(0,5)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=1.$ Hence, $q(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,5).$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$  is continuous on $[0,5]$ but has a corner point at $x=1.$  Hence, $r(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,5).$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, $s(x)$ is not continuous on $[0,5].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[0,5]$ but has corner points at $x=2$ and $x=4.$ Hence, $t(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2$ and $x=4,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,5).$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff51fe99-279c-5413-9a5e-c99560e4fa0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/83/83fffb6e46362e577cdc45bff6123555e070dc539505522ff134f2859954f9b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387776} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca4096f-e9bd-5d7c-b4e8-e94dec16e7ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5f/5f439fc57fa9cccf4471379d0b5a4901cdc4fd0ccd997fe9ae2e52d7c0372b94.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387777} {:answer/id #uuid "ea18565d-d69b-56e9-bde5-c6b30450c8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ffab62f3c9f42cd73ea6de5c9363de2218ba21fc0f53651c056494b47efc323a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387778} {:answer/id #uuid "390e1031-324c-5df0-b984-5ba28e768e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2e/2eba8ffb2094fab214cdebc0459ac6849c6bc2c37ac0f9bbd3fe026e44334800.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387779} {:answer/id #uuid "2cde7ea1-3a7d-5484-91cf-ccf7241e83a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c3/c30de46f9f22348af7a80c808ee8374fee195f44d3333c66c10803b42866a8cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cde7ea1-3a7d-5484-91cf-ccf7241e83a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c3/c30de46f9f22348af7a80c808ee8374fee195f44d3333c66c10803b42866a8cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387780} :answer-field/id #uuid "116621f2-14ab-56d4-9045-1d566ead4e89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "225ac492-a1e7-5d42-8e19-52b35451f042" :question/math-academy-id "q-127009" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$  Hence, it is not continuous on $x=0.$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[-2,2].$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $p(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$  Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebc29d59-7d86-5a0b-bfae-7dc8cf70f8a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/78/787772f6d68af4df373cbfdd6b7a3cf3d313db7e03ecce36437f01068b738408.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387783} {:answer/id #uuid "ecec397f-68c4-5168-adc5-fedad84e654a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4c/4c9f5d5245a1a4aa33a0d474ff328a30a1c8820055551e3613bf7c9489c2a908.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387784} {:answer/id #uuid "65530743-e7f0-5a82-b7c6-100bfaabb1c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d5927b5cc43ee1b5923b4d2aa4b4740e2bbc1c73b09b00525cb74767badc4808.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387785} {:answer/id #uuid "2a86b6b5-3c85-545c-9a4f-b4009d39a098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8d/8d37dc99a006ba89bc6a874d251a065fc714b260072740206f9cc3692b08c604.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387786} {:answer/id #uuid "6e362627-ed25-5cfe-949e-c9a97d23446f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f5/f5d7744d62f94b96b8bd1600ca7f5fc752d1f83c8c8928e740261e808cb9e07b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecec397f-68c4-5168-adc5-fedad84e654a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4c/4c9f5d5245a1a4aa33a0d474ff328a30a1c8820055551e3613bf7c9489c2a908.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387784} :answer-field/id #uuid "de026016-2f45-59c7-96dd-ec09795bc2b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48cd65ad-908c-58d2-aca2-a54d10434503" :question/math-academy-id "q-127011" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $m(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $n(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $n(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $l(x)$  has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $l(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$  has removable discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ae9599-1892-5be6-b1c8-b8ef620c7738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387790} {:answer/id #uuid "fca84d02-ccb4-52a9-a93c-7cd98c22b79f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387791} {:answer/id #uuid "dcddf5ad-bad0-5c48-9c2f-45b33927cb52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387792} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4fe1c6-8d45-519c-a36f-32e791441ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387793} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc8ff65-683e-5598-9183-38c4f3865f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcddf5ad-bad0-5c48-9c2f-45b33927cb52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387792} :answer-field/id #uuid "116b2669-f1fc-59d5-a844-fbb50e3ebbec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef077b10-897a-59ae-a756-7a254900bdb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-127068" :question/problem "Find the vector equation of the line $\\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z+3}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{7}\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(2,1,-3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨3,-2,7⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c400d85b-94d5-5fd2-98b4-472ab43c5be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28df22e1-dd03-5947-b0ac-b9a1f7c2c1e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387797} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c9481c-8fb4-5f53-acbc-cdca05453868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{17}⟨t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387798} {:answer/id #uuid "9d65ed45-2ec2-51f2-9a7e-ba5c68d47742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}⟨1,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387799} {:answer/id #uuid "f559334c-f8c3-5f1d-a130-8cbdd402370b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}⟨1,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387800} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4e9326-f664-59ef-be01-6624b9f86439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}⟨{t}^{2},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d65ed45-2ec2-51f2-9a7e-ba5c68d47742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}⟨1,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387799} :answer-field/id #uuid "199f3d52-77f3-5d4a-a683-3eb8e1bfd53d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfd26752-53ee-5373-a6ae-af80bab0a577" :question/math-academy-id "q-127217" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the unit tangent vector to the curve\n $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩$ at an arbitrary point where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),\\,y(t),\\,z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+{(-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64{cos}^{2}⁡2t+64{sin}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1+16({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1+16} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Finally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{17}}⟨2,\\,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}⟨1,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193b4900-4d07-53e2-be4e-48d5ec8d43ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387804} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8b69e0-aa2b-5483-90dc-48d46fb9dab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387805} {:answer/id #uuid "f829b1b0-0a47-5ec0-9116-1383df3f1616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387806} {:answer/id #uuid "74a4e07c-290d-5499-a014-7387d054ab73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387807} {:answer/id #uuid "83f0b0aa-1ec1-5f8f-9713-262b37261532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f8b69e0-aa2b-5483-90dc-48d46fb9dab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387805} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f87249-b6b1-5efb-aa0a-6f8127d0855d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3540ef43-cb67-51c1-8423-cebf06f99cf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127221" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a2f6f5b-f063-50fd-a445-e5206861484d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2{y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387811} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3a5b14-e539-50ca-b764-e8483d96d0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{2}(x+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387812} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2802b1-16eb-55bd-a33e-9c02fc8a176a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387813} {:answer/id #uuid "8051d521-18c9-5506-8dd8-4404b731cd23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}+12y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387814} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6e2d0e-132f-5112-af04-61afe3543d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff2802b1-16eb-55bd-a33e-9c02fc8a176a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387813} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7357b1f-7672-551e-bf04-066c16895432" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b4bd86f-3707-5569-a669-abddd19a5594" :question/math-academy-id "q-127228" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(3,0),(3,2),$ and $(0,2),$  traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+12xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/23/23066b23aebf332921c22a0439a414ade8dffd2a270641d186da0ed0e9488821.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=12xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x{y}^{2}+12x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2a9a1d-2d20-58cf-a1ee-bee2f4cf120d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Partitions of Large Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b329a959-25f6-594c-a659-8470f0f5903f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{{2, 4, 6, …, 40}, {80, 82, 84, …, 100}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056479} {:answer/id #uuid "05a12c9b-19c0-58c3-83ac-07f42792c8dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{{2, 4, 6, …, 40}, {20, 22, 24, …, 100}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056480} {:answer/id #uuid "20c2cd8c-801d-5198-91fa-9fe0e595df2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{{1, 2, 3, …, 100}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056481} {:answer/id #uuid "31e1b1de-9a85-5b7c-8a9b-28d6b984012e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{{4, 6, 8, 10}, {10, 11, 12, …, 100}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056482} {:answer/id #uuid "68d03eb0-1fb0-5e52-b292-125bdaf9e18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{{2, 4, 6, …, 40}, {42, 44, 46, …, 60}, {62, 64, 66, …, 100}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056483}] :answer-field/id #uuid "098a3924-da5d-5df5-8ff6-eecd2cf23e81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056478}] :question/id #uuid "7e7b9d16-36b0-577e-a63c-c84d780d74e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-1275" :question/problem "If $Y = {2, 4, 6, …, 100}$, which collection of subsets of $Y$ is a partition of $Y$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef73a00-12f7-5818-85a9-d8b5e2582bfe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f0e8c08-d49e-5e9e-92e7-8e442c4ed769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387818} {:answer/id #uuid "e953f65e-1d50-5014-b713-1e5c675deb94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨0,-\\sqrt{3-t},\\sqrt{t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387819} {:answer/id #uuid "114cb66e-ee19-5f24-9471-ea6f0a4c3ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387820} {:answer/id #uuid "9414d3f8-dd1b-571b-b827-ea037308b52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\sqrt{3-t},\\sqrt{t},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387821} {:answer/id #uuid "b58bd856-cf7c-5893-9c33-58c7df4a6936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b58bd856-cf7c-5893-9c33-58c7df4a6936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387822} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff4d1c9a-a300-541c-965c-7d03e73d4cf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "126d7092-f259-50a6-8d9e-e771713a98c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127613" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{t},0,\\sqrt{3-t}⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{3-t},0,-\\sqrt{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{t},0,\\sqrt{3-t}⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{3-t},0,-\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -\\sqrt{t} & 0 & \\sqrt{3-t} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3-t} & 0 & -\\sqrt{t}\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{3-t} \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{t} & \\sqrt{3-t} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3-t} & -\\sqrt{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{t} & 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3-t} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0-0,-(t+(3-t)),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d895a2-73eb-5141-b56d-48090debf57b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1693bed9-d0f0-55b1-b740-a527cbeb53ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+8{t}^{2}}}⟨2t,\\,2t,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387825} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a6ad77-6f89-543a-b273-f553d73a021e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387826} {:answer/id #uuid "3367f712-09e1-5ae1-8151-c97f6b2adc14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,\\,1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387827} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2576ba-96ef-5dd5-a88a-3c42695f8d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387828} {:answer/id #uuid "22a744b2-27a5-52ca-ad95-7d00b232171e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22a744b2-27a5-52ca-ad95-7d00b232171e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387829} :answer-field/id #uuid "b39d1a04-3d20-5a50-851c-2b9dd049f9b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9708eb7-d471-5841-8ae9-c412fc9cab47" :question/math-academy-id "q-127686" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨1,0,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4t}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}},\\,0,\\,\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(-2t)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩}{(\\frac{2}{1+4{t}^{2}})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨1,0,2t⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2t \\\\ -2t & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2t \\\\ -2t & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0-0,-(1+4{t}^{2}),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0,\\,-(1+4{t}^{2}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef2c4b9b-cf73-56d1-96b1-73f48b50ed7b" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrization of a Curve by Arc Length Given Its Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be979d2e-0b17-5d83-a44b-e9e9f493f578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387832} {:answer/id #uuid "63d8e15a-36c0-5d0e-abb6-acb136a98a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2s)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387833} {:answer/id #uuid "d61781f9-0300-5886-be20-7da50554e568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3s+2),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s+2)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387834} {:answer/id #uuid "172c4425-fc97-5b38-be53-1268b3920119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387835} {:answer/id #uuid "be6d94df-309d-5d79-a0fb-6c489908600c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3s-6),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s-6)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be979d2e-0b17-5d83-a44b-e9e9f493f578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387832} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7434be1-bf74-5c28-a679-abb576a9da2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3223e0f6-e1e4-5997-b282-51a0c4f828e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-127689" :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(t)=⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=6t.$ Find $R(s),$ the parametrization of the curve by its arc length $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=6t,$ we have that $t=\\frac{s}{6}.$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $r(t)$ by its arc length $s,$ with $s\\ge 0,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{6}) \\\\  & =⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{s}{6}),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{s}{6})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7343e3-fa7d-58c8-b4c7-49ef5b85110b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Tangent Given the Parametrization of a Curve by Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb62c734-f04b-55c4-8bd3-c4793e629968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{1}{5}⟨9,0,16⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387839} {:answer/id #uuid "310bb578-398b-5e0c-b06f-4a951396331b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387840} {:answer/id #uuid "7198b6f9-5ef1-533d-9afe-6ea7aa3d2357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387841} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0ea389-fa08-5116-b82c-ba029d1888b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}⟨3,1,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387842} {:answer/id #uuid "c5225ba2-26d0-562f-a151-05f4fab8d07e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨\\sqrt{3},1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7198b6f9-5ef1-533d-9afe-6ea7aa3d2357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387841} :answer-field/id #uuid "65ee013c-9eb7-52bb-bd68-12e344232014" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5d30766-fbf9-51ac-b32d-e1cc50064292" :question/math-academy-id "q-127709" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{3}{5}{t}^{3/2},\\,1,\\,\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{3/2}⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s={t}^{3/2},$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s={t}^{3/2},$ we have that $t={s}^{2/3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r({s}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{5}{({s}^{2/3})}^{3/2},\\,1,\\,\\frac{4}{5}{({s}^{2/3})}^{3/2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3s}{5},\\,1,\\,\\frac{4s}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parameterized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3s}{5}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4s}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{5},\\,0,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aecbb8b-bda3-5956-9b0f-98509330a84d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametrization of a Curve by Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a731427-7b07-5f24-89c3-30874476c839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s,\\,3,\\,-2s\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387846} {:answer/id #uuid "10a6c696-58d2-5ef6-9472-8be6af11b238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s},\\,3,\\,\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387847} {:answer/id #uuid "1a627ef4-c24c-5b1b-bddf-6512cf41eb1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨2s\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s,\\,3,\\,2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387848} {:answer/id #uuid "e79af7ed-b39f-54ce-8e12-881ba9d9b1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\frac{s}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}),\\,3,\\,\\frac{s}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387849} {:answer/id #uuid "2e26bff6-6ea0-5724-a3cc-0b6e736ef303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s},\\,3,\\,-\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10a6c696-58d2-5ef6-9472-8be6af11b238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s)=⟨\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s},\\,3,\\,\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387847} :answer-field/id #uuid "68b00312-bec7-5cbc-886e-bfa4bdaf0060" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "792ae83b-6f8e-58bb-84d1-2c821da6f452" :question/math-academy-id "q-127723" :question/problem "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3,\\,t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =|t| \\\\  & =t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},$ we have that $t=\\sqrt{2s}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\sqrt{2s}) \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s},\\,3,\\,\\sqrt{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2s}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2s}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74401a99-8151-5848-bfcc-1eef2569c204" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interval on Which a Product of Functions is Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7318932-e0c6-51b7-a0c4-cd65300b0d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387853} {:answer/id #uuid "af82498b-0801-536d-9436-97b28ae5d7f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387854} {:answer/id #uuid "60290bfa-538a-5bf7-9119-8c4b6ca14dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387855} {:answer/id #uuid "46c73bad-a7a4-5ae8-bff4-bafef104a6f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387856} {:answer/id #uuid "5e68b5c5-a037-51a1-8666-3cc3ddf6610e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46c73bad-a7a4-5ae8-bff4-bafef104a6f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387856} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b894de-3177-5235-bc2e-3df291bab42a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc73403e-607a-518c-9b45-2225317c88e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127879" :question/problem "Find the interval where the function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & ={e}^{2t+1}, \\\\ g(t) & =\\sqrt{t+4}\\,i+{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt[5]{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(t)=u(t)g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where both $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. The vector function $g(t)$ is differentiable on the common domain where its components are differentiable.  \n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. \n\nFor the real-valued function $u(t),$ we observe that it is differentiable over all values of $t.$\n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component, $\\sqrt{t+4}$,  is differentiable over $(-4,\\infty )$ since its derivative is not defined at $t=-4$ and the function is not defined for $t<-4.$\n\n- The second component,   ${sin}^{2}⁡t$,  is differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\n- The third component, $\\sqrt[5]{t}$, is differentiable over $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$\n\nTherefore, the intersection of the domains of all three components of  $g(t)$ is $(-4,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$  \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for $u(t)$ and $g(t)$ is $(-4,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0d93156-f9be-5e6e-976a-bef30dc892ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Is Parallel To a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28523b60-9c94-5e08-8811-eeb6c2c8385b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260235} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6f925d-2385-5203-beed-2ae656f27e99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260560} {:answer/id #uuid "71ffe7bc-2035-50b5-afb6-f098f001c957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260878} {:answer/id #uuid "c1024317-bb4d-5e9d-b670-c092e80227c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261584} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc3f903-0607-5523-bb23-e9a27fc580dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28523b60-9c94-5e08-8811-eeb6c2c8385b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260235} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e71e846-7ac8-5f2e-bf8c-340173700a84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1298261d-fd1d-57fd-8c0d-689d1739fab9" :question/math-academy-id "q-127888" :question/problem "A nonzero vector $⟨0,\\,b,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨3,2,-1⟩=\\sqrt{2}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨3,2,-1⟩=\\sqrt{2}$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨3,2,-1⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,2,-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}-{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & =3{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},\\,{t}_{2},\\,3{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{2}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{2}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45cdbb0d-2986-5c05-8d23-dce29363051b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Straight Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "901763ea-91a4-5ccc-9fd1-7a5abd771549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩=22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387860} {:answer/id #uuid "e471dd7f-cf5c-5449-bdd5-1669c5ee7ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,3,2⟩=14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387861} {:answer/id #uuid "be245f9d-2743-5776-a695-325f9e3f765e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387862} {:answer/id #uuid "e50391f5-f264-5d75-924b-8343f68bda37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,2,2⟩=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387863} {:answer/id #uuid "9c146846-53c1-5f64-8789-a2b060a469a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,6,2⟩=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be245f9d-2743-5776-a695-325f9e3f765e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387862} :answer-field/id #uuid "60a27afd-b25c-559e-94cd-93712349e320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78e0dc1f-5e12-54f0-b00c-492a979933d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-127937" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-1,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(-4,1,3)$ and $B(2,3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(-4,1,3)$ and $B(2,3,5)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,3,5⟩-⟨-4,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨6,2,2⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b54258-61d2-549c-94e4-a251f6d2a656" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a768cdba-762c-56fa-9c5a-e7c147a0278c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,\\,-2t,\\,2t-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387867} {:answer/id #uuid "308ec3e7-64ed-53d8-95f6-4019058929dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,2t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387868} {:answer/id #uuid "c6683669-2c03-5f5d-9b0c-ade45ed8142d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,\\,2t+1,\\,3{t}^{2}+2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387869} {:answer/id #uuid "76309ab8-5db5-5655-9fd2-0aa6a5daad69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,2t-2,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387870} {:answer/id #uuid "3051e681-37d6-5351-a17b-b57925e0ca74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,2t-2,\\,3{t}^{2}+2t-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3051e681-37d6-5351-a17b-b57925e0ca74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,2t-2,\\,3{t}^{2}+2t-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387871} :answer-field/id #uuid "437f6985-a5fd-5604-8d5e-718379fa984b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e49dfe3-04a3-591e-9ea0-48737f115840" :question/math-academy-id "q-127998" :question/problem "Differentiate $f(t)+g(t)$ with respect to $t$, given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨1-2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,(t-1)^{2}⟩, \\\\ g(t) & =⟨t+1,\\,1-2t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(f(t)+g(t))^{′}={f}^{′}(t)+{g}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-1)^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,2(t-1)⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f(t)+g(t))^{′} & ={f}^{′}(t)+{g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,2t,\\,2t-2⟩+⟨1,\\,-2,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2t-2,\\,3{t}^{2}+2t-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b54258-61d2-549c-94e4-a251f6d2a656" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a16c9dc1-0124-53fd-9b82-d496e53dbcff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-7,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387874} {:answer/id #uuid "f616ca61-be0f-5144-b6c8-08476269dd60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,-5,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387875} {:answer/id #uuid "309785b0-a118-5c93-829d-4b1bec8533d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,7,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387876} {:answer/id #uuid "a99725fb-c5ed-5e02-9f65-da1970e78632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,7,9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387877} {:answer/id #uuid "69eaec62-e0f1-50fe-ae12-fb1a693cb8d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-5,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69eaec62-e0f1-50fe-ae12-fb1a693cb8d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-5,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387878} :answer-field/id #uuid "f682f616-b62a-5bba-8f73-0577cd6f697d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "998ff16d-b6a6-5973-8b01-9ee14eef7c71" :question/math-academy-id "q-128144" :question/problem "Find the value of $(f(t)-2g(t))^{′}$ at $t=1,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩, \\\\ g(t) & =⟨2\\sqrt{t},\\,{t}^{3},\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n(f(t)-2g(t))^{′}={f}^{′}(t)-2{g}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t-1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f(1)-2g(1))^{′} & ={f}^{′}(1)-2{g}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =⟨2(1),\\,\\frac{1}{1},\\,{e}^{1-1}⟩-2⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}},\\,3(1)^{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩-2⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩-⟨2,6,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-5,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e8820fb-a6d0-5faf-9981-71cb5d041f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,-3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,-1,0⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387881} {:answer/id #uuid "6c78dd0f-845d-5bcb-9b44-79f85c755d47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,9,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨-7,0,1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387882} {:answer/id #uuid "d00473ec-47a7-55f6-8a63-b87befe37369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,6,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,7,1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387883} {:answer/id #uuid "d3744926-06d2-5a5f-a5e2-07abcc77fabc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,1,0⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387884} {:answer/id #uuid "47647d85-f672-5262-8d4f-f0d7a9258831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,-6,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,0,1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3744926-06d2-5a5f-a5e2-07abcc77fabc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,1,0⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387884} :answer-field/id #uuid "eab2fab9-a504-560f-bbc5-2ac638b9790f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a275fd86-a21a-5ef1-ae5c-9df806e069b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-128400" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $7x+y-3=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨7,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+y-3 & =0 \\\\ y & =3-7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$, we get $y=3.$ Hence, the point $P(0,3,0)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,3,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,1,0⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19f5f01-948e-5f44-8016-63073ed31dbc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Function Multiple Rule for Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb777dec-9a7d-5180-aed9-ced5ab07530a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {t}^{2}{e}^{-t}(3-t) \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387888} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8a4ef6-8622-5fc7-a443-ab29bf7a715d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3-t \\\\ {t}^{2}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387889} {:answer/id #uuid "36b3f452-c970-515d-8ba9-3c73dc8dd6d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ {t}^{2}{e}^{-t}(1-t) \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387890} {:answer/id #uuid "630e0a54-6d2c-509b-a321-bd2d769d03ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t{e}^{-t}(3-t) \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387891} {:answer/id #uuid "4add20c5-e378-54d6-93c5-3fa122ce7d58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ {t}^{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb777dec-9a7d-5180-aed9-ced5ab07530a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {t}^{2}{e}^{-t}(3-t) \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387888} :answer-field/id #uuid "33e6c637-4501-56e5-b8a0-e8e26bd20e58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b0680d9-f905-5e2d-ad7e-963cf0c87370" :question/math-academy-id "q-128488" :question/problem "Find ${(u(t)f(t))}^{′}$ where $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {t}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]$ and $u(t)={e}^{-t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)f(t))}^{′}={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})=-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)f(t))}^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {t}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -{t}^{3}{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}{e}^{-t}-{t}^{3}{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {t}^{2}{e}^{-t}(3-t) \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3061cb06-f57f-57c8-8821-dbd962b1e78e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bcab70b-517a-5271-894e-c4b561597fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,-2,7⟩+s⟨3,-1,5⟩+t⟨3,6,7⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387895} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4c8236-4299-5664-9863-74ff3a24010c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨7,2,3⟩+s⟨2,-1,2⟩+t⟨4,3,7⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387896} {:answer/id #uuid "37f487f1-ec69-5071-83c4-95ce287eda90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,-7,3⟩+s⟨2,-4,1⟩+t⟨3,5,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387897} {:answer/id #uuid "46c96bf2-16f7-52a0-a229-e72577f23ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨7,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,1⟩+t⟨3,4,7⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387898} {:answer/id #uuid "03dc462d-3599-5fba-aad0-834967006884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨7,-3,2⟩+s⟨3,-2,1⟩+t⟨7,4,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46c96bf2-16f7-52a0-a229-e72577f23ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨7,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,1⟩+t⟨3,4,7⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387898} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d29468-156d-5427-b6e3-354339252a04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d8e167e-ba7b-5746-8896-10f0f88087c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-128501" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $A(7,-2,3)$ and is parallel to the lines $r=⟨9,2,1⟩+s⟨2,-2,1⟩$ and $r=⟨4,-5,19⟩+t⟨3,4,7⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨2,-2,1⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨3,4,7⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $A(7,-2,3)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $a=⟨7,-2,3⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =a+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨7,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,1⟩+t⟨3,4,7⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "331fa12f-a113-5aba-bd6e-5bd5bf62298f" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Symbolic Statements Using the Associative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c760ec8-dd86-50a7-9b5c-d4d60417e53a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∨Q)∧R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387902} {:answer/id #uuid "01bb0013-0087-58d7-9ee4-4ac9b0f4647f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∧Q)∨R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387903} {:answer/id #uuid "ae83e118-c999-5a31-915a-5a05034d3396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∧Q)∧R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387904} {:answer/id #uuid "32dea5b0-8460-544c-8bed-39c3ad7ce727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q∨R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387905} {:answer/id #uuid "4b900d28-224c-597f-81d3-7235e9a375c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∨Q)∨R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b900d28-224c-597f-81d3-7235e9a375c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∨Q)∨R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387906} :answer-field/id #uuid "77264eff-e1c4-532d-bded-48c65fa90123" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44af959c-524d-5e05-a370-512c3479a5f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-128514" :question/problem "Simplify $P∨((P∨Q)∨R).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the associative and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∨((P∨Q)∨R) & ≡P∨(P∨(Q∨R)) \\\\  & ≡(P∨P)∨(Q∨R) \\\\  & ≡P∨(Q∨R) \\\\  & ≡(P∨Q)∨R.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a146d5d3-2562-5b8b-886e-4cf6519d3282" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Chain Rule for Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32740510-69e4-53a8-9cf8-9c29535d9249" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i-4{e}^{-2t}\\,j-2{e}^{-3t/2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387909} {:answer/id #uuid "10afedd6-ff7f-51fa-bc1d-464cb204e6b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,i-{e}^{-2t}\\,j-2{e}^{-t/2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387910} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb8b86f-001a-5eb1-9ded-dead76cc9891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,i-2{e}^{-t}\\,j-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-t/2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387911} {:answer/id #uuid "ea538a96-8b67-5fc4-ba6a-05f6aa91f674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,i-2{e}^{-2t}\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t/2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387912} {:answer/id #uuid "31024369-16ff-579a-acb7-e67065ff9581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,i-2{e}^{2t}\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{t}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea538a96-8b67-5fc4-ba6a-05f6aa91f674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,i-2{e}^{-2t}\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t/2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387912} :answer-field/id #uuid "93216f8a-001b-5ecf-87cd-938ff9643b14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "629c6742-cb38-552f-bd6e-09f3aee65a84" :question/math-academy-id "q-128561" :question/problem "If $f(u)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\,i+{u}^{2}\\,j+\\sqrt{u}\\,k$ and $u(t)={e}^{-t},$ then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡u)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({u}^{2})j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\sqrt{u})k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\,i+2u\\,j+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\,k \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})=-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{′}(u(t))\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{u(t)}\\,i+2u(t)\\,j+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u(t)}}\\,k)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{-t}}}\\,k)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =({e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{t/2}\\,k)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =-\\,i-2{e}^{-2t}\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t/2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a146d5d3-2562-5b8b-886e-4cf6519d3282" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Chain Rule for Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6770c6c3-ca44-5901-927f-1f6eb9c2f483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,-\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387916} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f467ee-f10f-5e09-b08f-f8641934f5aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387917} {:answer/id #uuid "375265a8-c029-5e99-9d9a-45d5e93a7d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387918} {:answer/id #uuid "78262439-79b2-52fc-a07a-ed5fa9d567e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{t},-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387919} {:answer/id #uuid "b4369e5c-dffe-55df-9352-d2f0194bfc84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-\\frac{t}{1+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "375265a8-c029-5e99-9d9a-45d5e93a7d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387918} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8592051-0273-5e7f-9289-65035199769c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00ec801b-e22a-5c19-899f-45b6911ab4a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-128570" :question/problem "If $g(u)=⟨\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+1),\\,1⟩$ and $u(t)=\\frac{1}{t}$, then $(g∘u)^{′}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(g∘u)^{′}(t)=g′(u(t))u′(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(u) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\frac{1}{u}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{u+1},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g∘u)^{′}(t) & =g′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(u(t))^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{u(t)+1},\\,0⟩\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{t}{1+t},\\,0⟩\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =⟨1,-\\frac{1}{t+{t}^{2}},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e18ca89-35ef-5360-b3a1-8c9496247abb" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Symbolic Statements Using the Commutative Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "727132d4-e01d-595c-81c6-b4576195dd6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387923} {:answer/id #uuid "c93e48c9-4983-5316-8f4b-ec6489bdfbb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q∧P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387924} {:answer/id #uuid "d394cac7-6e77-5434-803c-d90efef031fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(Q∨P)∧P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387925} {:answer/id #uuid "1de7ba6d-0971-58a7-9974-6f7019904bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387926} {:answer/id #uuid "f41388cc-767d-5b4c-a590-f4861d049e41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q∨P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f41388cc-767d-5b4c-a590-f4861d049e41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q∨P" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387927} :answer-field/id #uuid "836c305d-c91e-5702-aa6b-faae1fc85bcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4343a76b-e48f-576e-a60e-d3fc6f6f10d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-128622" :question/problem "Simplify $(P∨(Q∨P))∨P.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the associative, commutative, and idempotent laws, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∨(Q∨P))∨P & ≡(P∨(P∨Q))∨P \\\\  & ≡((P∨P)∨Q)∨P \\\\  & ≡(P∨Q)∨P \\\\  & ≡(Q∨P)∨P \\\\  & ≡Q∨(P∨P) \\\\  & ≡Q∨P.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5784bae8-d32b-522d-91c1-df798c1ad1d5" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Statement by Distributing a Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5675329-a94f-59c3-9bc6-dbc6b1d45c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬X∨Z)∧(¬X∨¬Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387930} {:answer/id #uuid "7d53aad9-18ff-5a12-8714-e6015b778e86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬X∧Z)∨(¬Y∧Z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387931} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ee8481-ebbf-5ccd-8c4c-437e416aaf5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬X∨Z)∧(¬Y∨Z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387932} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9e61f6-c819-596c-83ab-7c809551d3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬X∨Z)∨(¬Y∨Z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387933} {:answer/id #uuid "efc0f002-eec8-5cd8-bea1-4bb7c42607a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬X∨Z)∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7ee8481-ebbf-5ccd-8c4c-437e416aaf5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬X∨Z)∧(¬Y∨Z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387932} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffacee0d-1e7a-58c8-b3b2-967d3d8861fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f2febc6-8ef2-5626-b007-3fe5ab0095d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-128628" :question/problem "Which statement is equivalent to $(¬X∧¬Y)∨Z?$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute the disjunction over the conjunction, as follows:\n\n$$\n(¬X∧¬Y)∨Z≡(¬X∨Z)∧(¬Y∨Z)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c108451-a023-5057-8e13-628be2df85cf" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Statement by Distributing a Conjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29372d0d-bb5c-5ebb-a481-038b24d80a4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∨Q)∨(P∨¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387937} {:answer/id #uuid "530568ae-32f9-5bc6-9f6b-4c39ef84a753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∧Q)∧(P∧¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387938} {:answer/id #uuid "930d184c-6346-562d-aa22-1afcc8871f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∨Q)∧(P∨¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387939} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba2fd07-e47e-52f6-abfc-b38455d7da12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∧Q)∨¬R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387940} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8ed81f-a981-5bc4-99f0-a103c2e471d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∧Q)∨(P∧¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b8ed81f-a981-5bc4-99f0-a103c2e471d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∧Q)∨(P∧¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387941} :answer-field/id #uuid "052ba134-8e6c-54ff-8f20-8e0d8ae28a3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "664a3eba-03b4-5594-b97a-1b76f53d78d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-128632" :question/problem "Which statement is equivalent to $P∧(Q∨¬R)?$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute the conjunction over the disjunction, as follows:\n\n$$\nP∧(Q∨¬R)≡(P∧Q)∨(P∧¬R)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c108451-a023-5057-8e13-628be2df85cf" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Statement by Distributing a Conjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1919efd0-e983-5f6b-86ba-54252e25b636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z∨(Y∧¬X)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387944} {:answer/id #uuid "a56eae26-1d89-5c54-995b-8e3b74da1c7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬X∨Z)∨(Z∨Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387945} {:answer/id #uuid "6cea19dd-8411-5684-89d4-1fe413fef6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬X∨(Y∧Z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387946} {:answer/id #uuid "8eabbc94-4769-53a8-a2cf-a7289358934d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬X∧Z)∨(Z∧Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387947} {:answer/id #uuid "14b348c2-ca8e-5f16-8116-eedc800d3010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬X∧Z)∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eabbc94-4769-53a8-a2cf-a7289358934d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬X∧Z)∨(Z∧Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387947} :answer-field/id #uuid "2241d68e-617d-51eb-b7c1-52124d24a989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ecda4e45-7541-5589-bb81-6d7e4d5defd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-128638" :question/problem "Which statement is equivalent to $(¬X∨Y)∧Z?$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute the conjunction over the disjunction, as follows:\n\n$$\n(¬X∨Y)∧Z≡(¬X∧Z)∨(Y∧Z)\n$$\n\nNow, since $Y∧Z≡Z∧Y,$ we can write\n\n$$\n(¬X∧Z)∨(Y∧Z)≡(¬X∧Z)∨(Z∧Y).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44bf5c8e-cf2a-595c-b93a-5fe338ffd766" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Statement Using the Distributive Property for Conjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065e7912-b442-53ab-8229-dc087ababe53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬Z∨X)∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387951} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3511ec-0851-5864-ac5f-8b62508aac33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧(¬Z∨Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387952} {:answer/id #uuid "24649088-aed5-5e99-b6b3-74b28ad95e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬Z∧(X∨Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387953} {:answer/id #uuid "3eba303a-191a-5e77-a743-d4db1ee8d43d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧(¬Z∧Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387954} {:answer/id #uuid "aa09aaac-0ec8-53ed-a389-5cdb7105669c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∨(¬Z∨X)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb3511ec-0851-5864-ac5f-8b62508aac33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧(¬Z∨Y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387952} :answer-field/id #uuid "676cc675-6605-5c2c-9e4e-32f3ac4620f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9df95576-96db-5e7f-9cf6-c0fadd244c19" :question/math-academy-id "q-128644" :question/problem "Which statement is equivalent to $(X∧¬Z)∨(X∧Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can factor out the conjunction with $X,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n(X∧¬Z)∨(X∧Y)≡X∧(¬Z∨Y)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50c12386-8f44-5a50-af97-25cb87ae649f" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Statement Using the Distributive Property for Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb12013-ad2e-53a4-a2c3-3931692cf830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∨(Q∨¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387958} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc94c8a-c062-583c-8169-98cd657a41d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∧(Q∧¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387959} {:answer/id #uuid "2f91ceac-ec6a-5ac0-9f1e-de3b03cc756b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∨(¬Q∧¬¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387960} {:answer/id #uuid "27b634ec-0b27-5130-878e-8598e571627e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∨(Q∧¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387961} {:answer/id #uuid "7322cd8c-b290-5ef2-8547-9310995cea33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∧(Q∨¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27b634ec-0b27-5130-878e-8598e571627e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P∨(Q∧¬R)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387961} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6ce8cfd-2385-5069-885d-0df37da9dca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38bb30f4-3f50-5deb-9dfd-dd66eda5da6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-128651" :question/problem "Which statement is equivalent to $(¬P∨Q)∧(¬P∨¬R)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can factor out the disjunction with $¬P,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n(¬P∨Q)∧(¬P∨¬R)≡¬P∨(Q∧¬R)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d11f13f0-1cfa-59ec-a04a-9483d942b1aa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of the Plane Passing Through Three Given Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5688ffe4-a344-59de-904d-69d4ee154888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-1,-1⟩+t⟨3,5,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387965} {:answer/id #uuid "f1894930-5724-5ec7-9314-304ee3857bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨1,-2,-1⟩+t⟨5,3,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387966} {:answer/id #uuid "36f474e9-d65e-5772-a687-55109749a47b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-1,-2⟩+t⟨3,5,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387967} {:answer/id #uuid "cb27fd48-3d2d-5982-947b-b3e559f28c8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,1,2⟩+t⟨-3,1,5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387968} {:answer/id #uuid "17405e35-9e5b-5d7e-a665-511b494914e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,-1⟩+t⟨3,5,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17405e35-9e5b-5d7e-a665-511b494914e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,-1⟩+t⟨3,5,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387969} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b619d06-46ec-54c2-b23b-cc9a1e50c422" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8ae8a52-91d4-5f03-bbaf-4e5a85108c69" :question/math-academy-id "q-128741" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $P(1,-2,3),\\,Q(3,-4,2),$ and $R(4,3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-4,2⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =⟨4,3,2⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,5,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $p=⟨1,-2,3⟩$ of the point $P,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+s\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+t\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\ r & =⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,-1⟩+t⟨3,5,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d11f13f0-1cfa-59ec-a04a-9483d942b1aa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of the Plane Passing Through Three Given Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60c5c0f5-cea3-5255-8903-d2285b902635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨0,-1,-1⟩+t⟨2,-1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387972} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5a335a-8c39-5296-9221-80718d983791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨1,0,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387973} {:answer/id #uuid "a1feedd1-adb2-5c03-b2da-3a56801de734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨0,-1,0⟩+t⟨2,-3,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387974} {:answer/id #uuid "9a555223-84bf-5c63-a527-b176aedbb599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨1,0,-1⟩+t⟨-1,3,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387975} {:answer/id #uuid "0604c24b-d46b-5997-bc46-46a6a514d076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨1,-1,1⟩+t⟨3,-1,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60c5c0f5-cea3-5255-8903-d2285b902635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨0,-1,-1⟩+t⟨2,-1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387972} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f3ed1e-aca6-5323-9130-d1a84b4303b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0a7f0779-a02e-54b4-a490-a401988e7022" :question/math-academy-id "q-128745" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $D(3,2,-1),\\,E(3,1,-2),$ and $F(5,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =⟨3,1,-2⟩-⟨3,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =⟨5,1,2⟩-⟨3,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $d=⟨3,2,-1⟩$ of the point $D,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =d+s\\overset{\\to }{DE}+t\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\ r & =⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨0,-1,-1⟩+t⟨2,-1,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d54a6dbe-42cb-5614-ae0b-611de1719b45" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Product Rules for Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ec8602-0288-50f5-9393-d24f3e9978eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5{t}^{6},\\,{t}^{5}+4{t}^{2},\\,4{t}^{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387979} {:answer/id #uuid "059c4a79-58ba-5ab8-812b-f1ffa1abcd8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{6}-5,\\,4{t}^{5}+2t,\\,4{t}^{3}-10t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387980} {:answer/id #uuid "22face6d-6d51-591d-a1b9-744a0e476e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2{t}^{6}+1,\\,4{t}^{5},\\,{t}^{3}-5t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387981} {:answer/id #uuid "8428706c-8f90-591b-9287-63282825708a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-7{t}^{6}+5,\\,6{t}^{5}-2t,\\,4{t}^{3}-10t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387982} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb7b979-4cab-5af0-b6ed-092b5e887c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨7{t}^{6},\\,6{t}^{5}-5,\\,4{t}^{3}+2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8428706c-8f90-591b-9287-63282825708a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-7{t}^{6}+5,\\,6{t}^{5}-2t,\\,4{t}^{3}-10t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387982} :answer-field/id #uuid "658bfb28-3b3a-5fa6-937f-fc8138b0b72f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c21f1368-688b-50ed-bde8-7422d801bdbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-128896" :question/problem "Find $(f\\times g)^{′}(t)$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨t,\\,5,\\,{t}^{4}⟩, \\\\ g(t) & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{3},\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(f\\times g)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{2} & {t}^{3} & t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{2} & t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & {t}^{3}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =⟨-4{t}^{6},\\,4{t}^{5}-t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩, \\\\ f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ t & 5 & {t}^{4} \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & {t}^{4} \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}t & {t}^{4} \\\\ 2t & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}t & 5 \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =⟨5-3{t}^{6},\\,2{t}^{5}-t,\\,3{t}^{3}-10t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\times g)^{′}(t) & =⟨-4{t}^{6},\\,4{t}^{5}-t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩+⟨5-3{t}^{6},\\,2{t}^{5}-t,\\,3{t}^{3}-10t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7{t}^{6}+5,\\,6{t}^{5}-2t,\\,4{t}^{3}-10t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfd8982d-d859-5eb2-9f35-69af39c04822" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Level Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c206d91-b8f5-5a94-9316-75270a63bcd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c2c7ac29fce22a4448d286307dd3a87839c413f0a422cbdc2dbd13dc04144a7b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387986} {:answer/id #uuid "93f2eb5a-6350-5c8e-9435-c85bdfe64e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8a/8a631f2285c079c2effd4aea2c80fcf2f250a2c96f6ab11ca5b79961de5c3ce3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387987} {:answer/id #uuid "277bab86-7c6e-508e-9834-448c0cb1c29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7a/7aa8f113ff110ff76dba8a4bbb75eed0e3368c46a82eed25c00d63498c56f822.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387988} {:answer/id #uuid "b8bb77ca-8f07-5dac-beec-6bf48ca04c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/74db591aa317d90939685ef8a9f73c109c233b3f0f0cba971920ba4fcb1b4854.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387989} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fe9eb1-102d-5d7f-9c07-0688f68ddc05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/91fde8bc9eb57c1974e622a6673a030c9889c77d6788b620ffe70d7d6a8409c5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9fe9eb1-102d-5d7f-9c07-0688f68ddc05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/91fde8bc9eb57c1974e622a6673a030c9889c77d6788b620ffe70d7d6a8409c5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387990} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a1b305a-d716-5bd9-a834-19012dc96cc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e75a1d08-2fa1-51fa-b251-a3f4ae4ef9d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-129112" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=y-x?$" :question/worked-solution "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ y-x & =c \\\\ y & =x+c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of lines with slope of $1.$ Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/91/91fde8bc9eb57c1974e622a6673a030c9889c77d6788b620ffe70d7d6a8409c5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfd8982d-d859-5eb2-9f35-69af39c04822" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Level Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3138b86a-c727-542b-a704-b9f484f1e9c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/51/5155660bc0e9957b1a33cc6fbd933ba10618be07911f9384255bc703f7925ba7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387993} {:answer/id #uuid "61b4423f-aaa4-5e66-8f51-4bfffcff5496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/93/93cd9ed4c9c1507d1ff828abbb697cd72ab8ebdb7d44a1033d6e99a1eed9e633.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387994} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a86254-f752-5816-a65a-30478823f08b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0b/0bedc560b7b18c2a6841313d888dde7349b66f2514ce8c222af7ef2fc0bc9546.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387995} {:answer/id #uuid "78f3fc9b-3579-5680-8f2b-716e781df2f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c1/c115999648577bfb60015f0e54dd0c6d6de22076e87026ba94c49e56207144e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387996} {:answer/id #uuid "6684aa31-9bf2-51dd-b7a2-03fe9aa6f02f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d5e28121cd5847f3119a9fb33a57dd2d86e2f12bff3061ef64182b12c1ace178.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78f3fc9b-3579-5680-8f2b-716e781df2f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c1/c115999648577bfb60015f0e54dd0c6d6de22076e87026ba94c49e56207144e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387996} :answer-field/id #uuid "bace3461-26e7-51b1-ae89-c181bbb9a42d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "384942ae-d22a-5af8-bd31-df40d8002920" :question/math-academy-id "q-129116" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-x) & =c \\\\ y-x & ={e}^{c} \\\\ y & =x+{e}^{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of lines with slope of $1.$ Also, note that ${e}^{c}>0$ for all $c.$ Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/c1/c115999648577bfb60015f0e54dd0c6d6de22076e87026ba94c49e56207144e0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfd8982d-d859-5eb2-9f35-69af39c04822" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Level Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69ffca37-cace-5fde-98f2-279fdf4f3975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/56e923e4c1e2603c9bd3c621df0a5a4043fcc6a37ac73427be14541407fc19d8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388000} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef1d420-a12a-56ca-a25f-8624b9640b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/09f1a18e1a6fdcd29a40026cbffd8e8bdec23511bb692a1e920c18f942042470.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388001} {:answer/id #uuid "6719fa96-fc32-59ff-ad37-8586f87f620f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/67/67e9cfee7779fdde389a0804e7f52372ca9434de56b6611d296d4ac9c0759f72.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388002} {:answer/id #uuid "2c7de16e-8fae-51b2-b167-c594ccf39873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/aa/aa4d0cd189808c717648640c5543a632c099970a19d38d44298d409054f2627c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388003} {:answer/id #uuid "3995e781-cbde-5cfd-b61f-9c376d6d3f49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bf0f4b592cde648f63b21523aba1a49aeeb3b4dcaf909ba72c4629e088ca372a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6719fa96-fc32-59ff-ad37-8586f87f620f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/67/67e9cfee7779fdde389a0804e7f52372ca9434de56b6611d296d4ac9c0759f72.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388002} :answer-field/id #uuid "5053c438-eb23-5e9c-bd54-db15189721dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9ec437fa-7d05-53d2-8259-19727f28f519" :question/math-academy-id "q-129117" :question/problem "![](images/03/037422e7b1ce3b3d9067aba39c4fa85c6311770e63e70758c34aa61629c7f58e.png)\n\nWhich of the following could represent the level curves of the surface $z=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[-1,1].$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\in [-1,1].$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =c \\\\ y & =-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡c+2\\pi n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ can be any integer. \n\nNotice that the equations $y=-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡c+2\\pi n$ do not depend on $x.$ So, for a given $c\\in [-1,1]$ and integer $n$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of horizontal lines, as shown below.\n\n![](images/67/67e9cfee7779fdde389a0804e7f52372ca9434de56b6611d296d4ac9c0759f72.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0333b70a-2ede-5c8d-b298-4d25ae4d4667" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afc8add8-a972-543e-ba5d-502649377eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-12,\\,2\\sqrt{2}+1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388007} {:answer/id #uuid "88d76c60-f635-5b53-b9a2-21eb7b22c38d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-5,\\,\\sqrt{2}-2,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388008} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d13bc7-8fd9-5c74-85e1-35ca1c16399f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-10,\\,2\\sqrt{2}-6,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388009} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f9b0d5-b1ef-500e-9a9a-1756568b9eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,\\,5\\sqrt{2}+1,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388010} {:answer/id #uuid "a27359af-1ebf-57f7-a5f2-e6e3c036c4a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-12,\\,2\\sqrt{2}-1,\\,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a27359af-1ebf-57f7-a5f2-e6e3c036c4a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-12,\\,2\\sqrt{2}-1,\\,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388011} :answer-field/id #uuid "08002127-d475-5849-b316-13394942fb74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57f0244e-104a-5517-a9c5-a53620a2a15d" :question/math-academy-id "q-129149" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{3}[2f(t)+4g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-3,\\,\\sqrt{2},\\,2⟩$ and ${\\int }_{0}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-\\frac{3}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "From the vector addition and scalar multiplication rules, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}[2f(t)+4g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t=2{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+4{\\int }_{0}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[2f(t)+4g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+4{\\int }_{0}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2⟨-3,\\,\\sqrt{2},\\,2⟩+4⟨-\\frac{3}{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{4},\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,\\,2\\sqrt{2},\\,4⟩+⟨-6,\\,-1,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-12,\\,2\\sqrt{2}-1,\\,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d469811-ad49-51b7-ba98-f36dda7fa8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-4,6,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388014} {:answer/id #uuid "55c95756-c3ad-51e7-bfd2-ff829b2645f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,-4,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388015} {:answer/id #uuid "7db145f2-bd3e-56ff-83a7-ec3ad48ffafe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,6,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388016} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ecd231-43e4-5db5-9f1d-b84553a750df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-6,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388017} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7b4075-2dcb-5db3-95e3-edb197f4913a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,4,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7db145f2-bd3e-56ff-83a7-ec3ad48ffafe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,6,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388016} :answer-field/id #uuid "38007ff1-e837-5245-a985-db1b0cc61194" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0aedac8-4550-5f55-b4ac-09ba9ac8ed13" :question/math-academy-id "q-129169" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+3z-11=0 \\\\ 2x-2y-5=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+3z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,3⟩\\times ⟨2,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+6j-4k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "907f2c4f-a7e3-593c-bb46-4fc277b70d5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,10⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388021} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2a73d2-b934-58fd-8801-1cf079a8a792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,15,10⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388022} {:answer/id #uuid "be16c1e5-86fe-53f6-b99a-b5cf526b20f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨15,4,10⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388023} {:answer/id #uuid "c422581d-9fb0-51ca-8752-a801273ce3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,6,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388024} {:answer/id #uuid "e459a854-7a51-5fab-8a48-995a7c6acd9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨14,11,10⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c2a73d2-b934-58fd-8801-1cf079a8a792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,15,10⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388022} :answer-field/id #uuid "1307634c-0843-58dc-9882-c76fe53c9f6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85fcd4d1-25d1-559e-b810-7404c2aacc48" :question/math-academy-id "q-129171" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-2z+13=0 \\\\ 2y-3z-25=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $5x-2z+13=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨5,0,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z-25=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨5,0,-2⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+15j+10k \\\\  & =⟨4,15,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9137657-ea64-55ac-a50c-776389fe269a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388028} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb2d8a9-4b43-5c1d-a4f4-5266329e7446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388029} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb29629-4b1b-5406-ae1b-3f10adcfde85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388030} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5bcfcc-b800-5ca5-aba0-22a1220a9fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388031} {:answer/id #uuid "9b00eaee-d865-5c36-ba00-8bc6c7e12db0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-2,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbb29629-4b1b-5406-ae1b-3f10adcfde85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388030} :answer-field/id #uuid "8224c570-9b5e-580f-8450-ee7ed8cf16e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c61ebc2f-8bbc-5c89-bcf3-6676fa54cd9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129174" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the planes $2x=6$ and $z=1-y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $2x=6$, a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=1-y$ (or $y+z=1$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eb7fd4f-f4bc-535e-b6bf-0c7df387a375" :knowledge-point/title "Reversing the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6088ac3-62a3-55f9-9254-bd3af6af7de0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-2,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388035} {:answer/id #uuid "a26c5fac-577d-55ea-8c59-6e164ae1455b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388036} {:answer/id #uuid "bb49ba41-a2a8-57f8-98f2-980a02993f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,\\,4,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388037} {:answer/id #uuid "adc83598-1819-58a2-a620-584ae08d2974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-2,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388038} {:answer/id #uuid "c06243b2-433d-5357-a811-c37305881773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-4,\\,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6088ac3-62a3-55f9-9254-bd3af6af7de0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-2,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388035} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df1086a-a10d-51ec-be27-8b1ada17a1b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b77f59d-ad05-5d0e-a768-18e851546b1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129266" :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)+⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-1,\\,3,\\,-2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-⟨-1,\\,3,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-3,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the vector addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)+⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{0}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-3,\\,2⟩+⟨t,\\,-t,\\,0⟩{|}_{1}^{0} \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-3,\\,2⟩+⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\text{ } \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5287f63-6d1a-5710-8a71-df5d1ba09a00" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Adjacent Intervals Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c21f973b-6827-55c5-baa5-2565f439cc1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388042} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b8f243-9834-546b-8903-9d353fcc0cea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-4,\\,2,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388043} {:answer/id #uuid "21f5c299-ab2b-57d3-bd55-6cc1ec25156d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,-2,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388044} {:answer/id #uuid "40c6620e-4536-587f-89d2-39981f1c9edf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-9,\\,2,\\,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388045} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5e8b0e-193a-5ee6-a1ea-d5a55a07f580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,\\,1,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21f5c299-ab2b-57d3-bd55-6cc1ec25156d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,-2,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388044} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6776540-2b3d-5297-905d-0e8c15e5c16c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cec9543d-3be0-5419-a7dd-c01379498092" :question/math-academy-id "q-129269" :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨8,\\,-4,\\,4⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{3}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨4,\\,-2,\\,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the adjacent intervals rule, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nFinally, using the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,-4,\\,4⟩-⟨4,\\,-2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-2,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5287f63-6d1a-5710-8a71-df5d1ba09a00" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Adjacent Intervals Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "267663dd-055e-5e02-abff-4b68a439b8bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,6,\\,-11⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388049} {:answer/id #uuid "fa3cb164-8d83-5780-8868-78fe2f7bee03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-2,\\,11⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388050} {:answer/id #uuid "746a4fc2-1838-504a-906f-bf0e5cd46b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,2,\\,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388051} {:answer/id #uuid "c26d5c6a-21e5-5524-adc1-f87c56b3dd55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,2,\\,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388052} {:answer/id #uuid "8370fd13-a693-5e66-bb34-e94b11fd1fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,\\,-1,\\,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "267663dd-055e-5e02-abff-4b68a439b8bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,6,\\,-11⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388049} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f4d56ea-a562-514d-8f5a-e9001c6a209a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78c4a8a8-5c24-5d9d-99b9-eb62aeea153c" :question/math-academy-id "q-129272" :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨2,\\,4,\\,-10⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the adjacent intervals rule, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nFinally, using the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-(-{\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,4,\\,-10⟩+⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,6,\\,-11⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c7a77cf-163a-542a-b4a3-4274344b9cf8" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying 3x3 Square Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3e9d798-fb90-59d5-a203-1417449a2049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-14 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 14 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388056} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ba9d1d-813c-5106-b50d-217b417a01b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 14 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388057} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee9a11d-be69-54bb-9606-cb4e7fe20b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 14 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db